,title,link,text,language,family
0,Byangsi language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Byangsi_language,"Byangsi is not written, although there has been a recent movement among its speakers to create a script for a uniform written language, which may greatly help preserve the language for years to come if successful.",Byangsi,Sino-Tibetan
1,Music of Tibet - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_Tibet,"The soundtrack to Kundun, by Philip Glass, has helped to popularize Tibetan music. Foreign styles of popular music have also had a major impact within the Tibetan diaspora, where Indian ghazal and filmi are very popular and American rock has produced the India-based Rangzen Shonu.",Byangsi,Sino-Tibetan
2,Sino-Tibetan languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-Tibetan_languages,"Sino-Tibetan (also referred to as Trans-Himalayan) is a family of more than 400 languages, second only to Indo-European in number of native speakers. Around 1.4 billion people speak a Sino-Tibetan language. The vast majority of these are the 1.3 billion native speakers of Sinitic languages.",Byangsi,Sino-Tibetan
3,"Tibetan Music | Himalayan Temple | Instrumental Asian MusicThe best Tibetan songs all time. - YouTube MusicThe traditional music of the Qiang, Pumi and Lisu ... - 資訊咖Byangsi language - WikipediaRhythms of Heritage: Enchanting “Xianzi” Performance at the ...Music of Tibet - WikipediaBuddhaNet Audio: Chanting - buddhanet.net",https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=J9dAmDy3jWE,"Dec 22, 2017 · Relaxing music for listening while you work, study, read, relax or sleep. From world music to instrumental piano and lofi/chill. Listen to The best Tibetan songs all time. on YouTube Music - a dedicated music app with official songs, music videos, remixes, covers, and more. May 27, 2026 · The Sino-Tibetan language family is one of the largest and most dynamic language families in the world, with more than one billion speakers. What I want to introduce today is the traditional music of the Qiang, Pumi and Lisu people under the Sino-Tibetan language family. Byangsi is not written, although there has been a recent movement among its speakers to create a script for a uniform written language, which may greatly help preserve the language for years to come if successful. Mar 9, 2025 · The enchanting melodies of “Xianzi” (a traditional Tibetan string instrument) performance during the Tibetan New Year Gala embodies the essence of Tibetan intangible heritage and the festive joy. The soundtrack to Kundun, by Philip Glass, has helped to popularize Tibetan music. Foreign styles of popular music have also had a major impact within the Tibetan diaspora, where Indian ghazal and filmi are very popular and American rock has produced the India-based Rangzen Shonu. This is an offering of Buddhist Chanting. Eventually we hope it will represent devotional and sutra chanting from all Buddhist traditions. If you have any quality audio material to contribute to this section (especially Tibetan), it would be much appreciated. Please email: webmaster. Courtesy of Phor Kark See Monastery, Singapore. Why is Tibetan music considered religious music? The religious music of Tibet reflects the profound influence of Tibetan Buddhism on the culture. The new-age ' singing bowl ' music marketed in the West as 'Tibetan music' is of 1970s US origin. Western research into the history of Tibetan music has often focused more on religious than secular musics. What are tibetan'street songs'? Tibetan ""street songs"" were a traditional form of expression particularly popular as a means of political and other commentary in a country that was previously without newspapers or other means of mass communication. Where is Byangsi spoken? Byangsi is part of a group of four small West Himalayish languages spoken in the former ""state of Almora"", now divided between India's Uttarakhand and far-western Nepal, viz., Rangkas, Darmiya, Chaudangsi and Byangsi. Who are the Byangsi people? The speakers of Byangsi believe themselves to be descended from the Darchula Byangsi people, high-caste Parbatiya, and Tibetans. Until recently, the hill region where the people live was closed to foreign researchers, so very little information has been gathered on the languages of the area.",Byangsi,Sino-Tibetan
